  "cli_result_text": "Files submitted, ordered by relevance to the exception-provenance trace:\n\n1. **`HttpWebHandlerAdapter.java`** (spring-web) \u2014 the actual point where context is lost: `handleUnresolvedError()` collapses any unhandled `Throwable` into a bare `HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR` with no body detail, logs only via `logger.error(...)`, and even special-cases disconnected-client exceptions to return `Mono.empty()` silently.\n2. **`ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java`** (spring-web) \u2014 the bridge that adapts reactor-netty's `HttpServerRequest/HttpServerResponse` handling function into Spring's `HttpHandler`; catches only `URISyntaxException` explicitly and otherwise just logs errors via `.doOnError(...)` without altering how the underlying exception propagates.\n3. **`AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java`** (netty) \u2014 implements `fireExceptionCaught`, propagating exceptions along the pipeline context-to-context.\n4. **`DefaultChannelPipeline.java`** (netty) \u2014 the pipeline's tail handler behavior when no handler in the chain calls `exceptionCaught`; this is where an unhandled exception in the pipeline gets logged and effectively dropped if no downstream handler acts on it.\n5. **`WebExceptionHandler.java`** (spring-web) \u2014 the WebFlux contract for exception handling (`handle(exchange, ex)`), showing what a properly wired handler *should* look like vs. the fallback path in `HttpWebHandlerAdapter`.\n6. **`ReactorServerHttpResponse.java`** (spring-web) \u2014 adapts `ServerHttpResponse` onto reactor-netty's `HttpServerResponse`; relevant to how status codes/headers actually get written (or not) once an error reaches this layer.\n7. **`ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter.java`** (netty) \u2014 shows the default `exceptionCaught` just forwards via `ctx.fireExceptionCaught(cause)`, illustrating how a handler that doesn't override this method passes the exception along unchanged (and unenriched).\n8. **`SslHandler.java`** (netty) \u2014 one of the concrete pipeline handlers that catches and re-raises exceptions (`exceptionCaught(ctx, wrapIfNeeded(cause))`), relevant to connection-reset scenarios at the TLS layer.\n9. **`ChannelInitializer.java`** (netty) \u2014 another concrete `exceptionCaught` call site, relevant to pipeline setup-time exceptions.",
